<!--
  Custom Wazuh detection rule — targets log tampering / anti-forensics.

  Finding: the generic FIM rule 550 ("Integrity checksum changed") fires
  identically whether a monitored file grows, shrinks, or just has its
  content edited. A SIZE change specifically to /var/log/auth.log is
  the signal worth escalating — a healthy auth.log being tampered with
  (truncated or rewritten) will show "size" in its changed_attributes.

  Design (v2, corrected after live testing): the first version of this
  rule tried to approximate a numeric "before < after" comparison using
  digit-count regexes on size_before/size_after. That approach never
  fired reliably in testing — FIM's own baseline-timing behavior (see
  writeup #4) meant the actual before/after pair at the moment of a real
  truncation rarely lined up with the guessed digit-count thresholds.

  The fix: stop guessing at numeric comparison entirely. Wazuh's syscheck
  decoder already exposes which attributes changed as a list
  (changed_attributes: ["size", "mtime", "md5", ...]). Checking whether
  "size" is literally in that list — via the <field name="changed_fields">
  condition below — is exact, not approximate, and matches regardless of
  which direction the size moved or by how much.

  Field names used (confirmed against a real fired alert's JSON):
  - "file"          matches the monitored path (regex-anchored to
                     /var/log/auth.log specifically, so this rule doesn't
                     fire for every other file under the now-broadened
                     /var/log FIM scope)
  - "changed_fields" matches against the changed_attributes list; "size"
                     here means a size change occurred, in either direction

  Prerequisite: /var/log must be added to the agent's <syscheck>
  directories (realtime="yes") and the default `.log$` ignore pattern in
  <ignore type="sregex"> narrowed, otherwise log files are never watched
  by FIM at all — auth.log is excluded from monitoring by default.

  Install: place in /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml on the MANAGER,
  then `sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager`.

  Status: CONFIRMED LIVE. Fired as rule 100101, level 12, on a real
  truncation test against /var/log/auth.log (size_before: 81,
  size_after: 386 — the post-truncate "self-logging floor" growth
  described in writeup #4, not the literal 0; see that writeup for why).
  Alert id 1795118807.45953, firedtimes: 5 at time of confirmation.

  Known limitation (documented, not hidden): this rule matches a size
  change in EITHER direction, since changed_fields doesn't carry sign
  or magnitude. Normal log growth (a legitimate new auth.log entry that
  happens to be large enough to register as a "size" change) can also
  trigger it. In practice this still has clear triage value — every hit
  is a real auth.log size event worth a human glance, and the two real
  attack actions in writeup #4 both pass through it — but it is a
  broader net than a true "sharp drop only" detector would be. A future
  refinement would correlate this with the actual byte delta or with
  nearby sudo/PAM events rather than firing on changed_fields alone.
-->
<rule id="100101" level="12">
  <if_sid>550</if_sid>
  <field name="file">^/var/log/auth\.log$</field>
  <field name="changed_fields">size</field>
  <description>auth.log size changed - investigate possible log tampering</description>
  <mitre>
    <id>T1070.002</id>
  </mitre>
  <group>local,syslog,sshd,log_tampering,defense_evasion,</group>
</rule>
